@database "ssh_control"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: ssh_control.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "SSH Control"
@{h1}SSH Control

An agent that looks after servers runs commands on them over SSH: it
checks a disk, restarts a service, starts a long build in the background
and reads its output later, copies files to and from a machine. The
machines are named in the configuration -- an agent addresses
@{tt}build-host@{utt}, not an address and a key. In the @{b}SSH
Machines@{ub} panel you see every machine with its state and what was run
there, run commands yourself, and add or remove machines.

This plugin gives an agent a shell on other computers. Read
@{" What an agent can do " link security} before you give it to one.

@{image docs/panel.png "The SSH Machines panel with the history of a machine"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The SSH Machines panel  " link panel}
  @{"  What an agent can do  " link security}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Running commands  " link execute}
  @{"  Background commands  " link background}
  @{"  Files  " link files}
  @{"  Machines and connections  " link machines}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The SSH Machines panel"
@{h1}The SSH Machines panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}System@{ub}, "SSH Machines"). It answers
administrators only: every other role gets the server's refusal.

@{image docs/panel.png "The SSH Machines panel"}

The panel shows the machines of the plugin, whichever chat is open --
they are not tied to a session.

@{h2}The machines

One tab per machine, with a dot for its state:

@{table}
State | Means
green, with milliseconds | used in the last five minutes; the time is the last measured round trip
Connected | used in the last five minutes, no round trip measured yet
Not connected yet | nothing has connected to it since the server started; the first command does
Idle | connected to before, not used in the last five minutes
Unreachable (red) | the ping of a click on reload failed; the reason is shown next to it until the next tick
@{body}

The list reloads every 5 seconds (the menu next to the reload button sets
another interval or turns it off). A tick only looks at when each machine
was last used and never connects. A click on the reload button pings the
machines connected to before; a machine never connected to is not
connected to for it, and one whose connections are all busy is judged by
its last use instead of being waited for.

Above the output: @{tt}user\@host:port@{utt}, the state, the tags and
@{b}Remove@{ub}.

@{h2}The output

What was run on the machine, the last 50 runs in the order they were
recorded: the command,
its output, its errors in red, and a line with the exit code (or why it did
not run to the end), the duration and the time. A run that failed is drawn
in red; an output longer than 10 KB is cut there and says so. The
history holds the agents' commands and the panel's alike, background
commands once they have ended, and failed ones too. A background command
is recorded when it ends but shows the time it started, so it can sit
below runs that started after it. It lives in the
server's memory -- 1000 runs over all machines -- and is gone after a
restart.

@{h2}Running a command

Type a command after the @{tt}name$@{utt} prompt and press @{b}Run@{ub}
or Enter. It runs as the machine's user, in that user's shell, with the
machine's command timeout (300 seconds unless configured otherwise). The
line is locked and the command shown with a spinner until it is done; the
output then appears in the history. A machine that cannot be reached, or a
command that does not finish in time, is shown as an error and is in the
history as well. There is no background mode here: a command that takes
longer than the timeout is cut off.

@{h2}Adding a machine

@{b}Add machine@{ub} asks for a name, host, port, username and the way
to log in: an SSH key (a key file on the server, @{tt}~/.ssh/id_rsa@{utt}
unless you give another), a password, or the SSH agent of the server.
The connection is tested first; a refusal -- no connection within 10
seconds, a host key that is not trusted, a login that fails -- is shown in
the dialog and nothing is added. @{b}Keep across restarts@{ub} stores the
machine without any password (see @{" Machines and connections " link machines});
a machine with a password is added but not kept, and the panel says so.

@{h2}Removing a machine

@{b}Remove@{ub} asks first, closes the machine's connections and removes
it from the stored machines too. A machine from the configuration comes
back at the next start; the panel says so. A command already running on
it is cut off with its connection.
@endnode

@node security "What an agent can do"
@{h1}What an agent can do

@{h2}Commands

There is no command allowlist. An agent that has @{tt}execute@{utt} runs
any command line, as the machine's user, in that user's shell -- pipes,
@{tt}sudo@{utt} and all. What limits it is that account on that host:
give the plugin an account that can do what the agent should do and no
more.

Only @{tt}command@{utt} is a shell line. Every other parameter -- machine
names, paths, modes, process ids -- is used as a value: paths go over
SFTP, never through a shell, and a machine name picks a configured machine
or is refused.

@{h2}Hosts

An agent reaches only machines by name: an unknown name is refused with
the list of known ones. But @{tt}add_machine@{utt} lets it add any host,
with any user, any key file the server can read (@{tt}~/.ssh/id_rsa@{utt}
when it names none) or the server's SSH agent. Two things bound that:

@{bullet}With strict host key checking (the default) a host must already
be in the server's known_hosts file, or the connection test fails with
"Host key is not trusted for host ..." and nothing is added.
@{bullet}The tools can be allowed one by one. An agent that should work
only on the configured machines gets no @{tt}add_machine@{utt} and no
@{tt}remove_machine@{utt} (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}).

@{tt}remove_machine@{utt} can take a configured machine away until the
next start, and a machine of that name can then be added with another
host.

@{h2}Local files

@{tt}upload_file@{utt} reads and @{tt}download_file@{utt} writes local
files only inside one folder, the setting @{tt}local_root@{utt} (see
@{" Setting it up " link setup}). Without it both tools refuse every call.
A relative @{tt}local_path@{utt} starts in that folder, also one
beginning with @{tt}data/@{utt}; an absolute one
must lie in it.

The path is resolved before it is checked: @{tt}..@{utt} and symlinks or
junctions that lead out of the folder are refused, and so is a download
onto a link to a file outside. A host path (@{tt}\\\\host\\share@{utt},
@{tt}//host/share@{utt}) is refused from its text alone -- Windows would
otherwise connect to the host and sign in with the server's credentials
before any check. A folder, a Windows device name (@{tt}CON@{utt},
@{tt}COM1@{utt}, ...) and a path starting with @{tt}~@{utt} (not expanded)
are refused as @{tt}local_path@{utt} too.

Inside the folder there is no further limit: a download replaces a file
that is there. An agent that should not move files between the server
and a machine at all gets neither tool.

@{h2}Host keys

@{tt}strict_host_key_checking@{utt} is on unless the configuration sets
it to @{tt}false@{utt} -- an empty value keeps it on. Then every connection checks the host's key against
@{tt}known_hosts_file@{utt} (@{tt}~/.ssh/known_hosts@{utt}). If that file
does not exist, or the setting is empty, no connection is made -- the
plugin never falls back to accepting any key. Turned off, every key is
accepted: anyone who can take over the address gets the commands and the
password.

@{h2}Credentials

Passwords and passphrases never appear in a tool answer or in the panel;
@{tt}list_machines@{utt} shows the login method only, and a key path
shows only in the error that its file is missing. A
password in the configuration can come from the environment:
@{tt}password: "${BUILD_HOST_PASSWORD}"@{utt}. A password an agent passes
to @{tt}add_machine@{utt} is part of the conversation -- the tool call's
arguments -- and stays in the server's memory; it is never written to the
machine store. Key or agent logins keep secrets out of both.

Whatever the login method, asyncssh also offers the keys of the server's
SSH agent (@{tt}SSH_AUTH_SOCK@{utt}) and the default keys
@{tt}~/.ssh/id_*@{utt} of the account the server runs as. A machine set
up for a password or one key may therefore log in with another key that
account holds.

@{h2}The SSH client configuration

asyncssh reads @{tt}~/.ssh/config@{utt} of the account the server runs
as. A @{tt}ProxyCommand@{utt} or @{tt}Match exec@{utt} there that uses
@{tt}%h@{utt} runs a local command with the host name in it -- and with
@{tt}add_machine@{utt} an agent chooses that host name. Keep such entries
out of that account's configuration, or keep @{tt}add_machine@{utt} from
the agent.

@{h2}The audit trail

With @{tt}security.audit_log@{utt} on (the default), every command that
reached a machine -- finished, failed, timed out or cancelled --, every background
command started and every file copied writes a line to the server's
log:

@{code}
AUDIT: {"timestamp": "2026-09-29 10:15:02", "operation": "execute_command",
        "machine": "build-host", "command": "df -h /", "exit_code": 0, "duration": 0.21}
@{body}

The operations are @{tt}execute_command@{utt} (with @{tt}error@{utt}
instead of the exit code when it failed), @{tt}start_background@{utt}
(with the @{tt}process_id@{utt}), @{tt}upload_file@{utt} and
@{tt}download_file@{utt}. The command is logged as sent, so a secret typed
into a command is in the log. There is no separate audit file.

@{h2}Who can use it

The panel answers administrators only (a rule for
@{tt}/plugins/ssh_control/*@{utt} in @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt}).
The tools reach every agent whose allowlist names them; limit the agent
itself to administrators with @{tt}min_role: admin@{utt} in its metadata.
Background commands belong to the session that started them: another
session can neither read nor stop them, nor take over their
@{tt}process_id@{utt}.
@endnode

@node execute "Running commands"
@{h1}Running commands

The tools are named after the plugin instance: @{tt}ssh_control_execute@{utt}
and so on in the default setup.

@{tt}ssh_control_execute@{utt} runs a command on one machine and answers
when it is done.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
machine | the machine's name; required
command | the command line; required
timeout | seconds; without it the machine's command_timeout (300 by default)
check_exit_code | true (default): a non-zero exit counts as failed; false: it counts as success
background | true: start it and return at once, see @{" Background commands " link background}
wake, process_id | background commands only
@{body}

@{h2}The answer

@{code json}
{"results": [{"machine": "build-host", "command": "df -h /",
              "stdout": "Filesystem ...", "stderr": "", "exit_code": 0,
              "duration": 0.21, "success": true}],
 "total_machines": 1, "successful": 1, "failed": 0}
@{body}

A non-zero exit with @{tt}check_exit_code@{utt} on adds
@{tt}error: "Command failed with exit code 2"@{utt}. A command that did not
run to the end has @{tt}error@{utt} and @{tt}success: false@{utt} and no
output: "Timed out after 300s", "Unknown machine: x. Available: [...]",
the connection's failure, or "No connection slot available to ... within
30.0s" when every connection of the machine stays busy for 30 seconds.
Stdout and stderr come back whole; a command with a lot of output should
be cut on the machine (@{tt}| tail -50@{utt}). On a timeout the plugin
stops waiting and sends no signal: the command may go on running on the
host.

A missing @{tt}machine@{utt} or @{tt}command@{utt} fails the call with an
error. @{tt}wake@{utt} on a foreground command answers @{tt}wake:
false@{utt} with a note: the results are already there.
@endnode

@node background "Background commands"
@{h1}Background commands

A build, a backup, a long download: @{tt}execute@{utt} with
@{tt}background: true@{utt} starts the command and answers as soon as it
runs.

@{code json}
{"machine": "build-host", "command": "make all", "background": true, "wake": true}
{"status": "success", "process_id": "ssh_proc_3f9a1c2e", "machine": "build-host",
 "command": "make all", "started_at": "2026-09-29T10:15:02", "wake": true}
@{body}

@{h2}Limits

@{bullet}One machine per command, and no timeout: it runs until it ends or
is stopped.
@{bullet}It holds one connection of the machine for its whole run, and one
connection always stays free for ordinary commands: a machine with
@{tt}max_connections: 3@{utt} runs two background commands at a time, one
with 1 runs none. A third answers @{tt}error_type:
BackgroundLimitReached@{utt}.
@{bullet}Each stream keeps its last 1000 lines. Of the finished commands
the 50 that ended last stay readable.
@{bullet}They live in the server's memory. A restart or a stop of the
server stops them.

@{h2}process_id

Without one the plugin makes an id (@{tt}ssh_proc_@{utt} and eight hex
digits). A given id may be reused once its command has ended; one still
running answers @{tt}ProcessIdInUse@{utt}, and so does an id of another
session.

@{h2}Reading and stopping

@{tt}ssh_control_get_output@{utt} takes @{tt}process_id@{utt},
@{tt}stream@{utt} (stdout, stderr or both, the default) and
@{tt}clear_buffer@{utt} (true drops what was read, so the next read gets
only what is new). It answers @{tt}stdout@{utt}, @{tt}stderr@{utt},
@{tt}is_running@{utt}, @{tt}exit_code@{utt} (null while it runs),
@{tt}started_at@{utt}, @{tt}finished_at@{utt} and @{tt}error@{utt}.

@{tt}ssh_control_kill_process@{utt} takes @{tt}process_id@{utt} and
@{tt}force@{utt} (false: SIGTERM, true: SIGKILL) and answers with the
@{tt}signal@{utt} sent -- @{tt}none@{utt} with a note when the command
had already ended or had not started yet.

An unknown id, or one of another session, answers @{tt}{"status":
"error", "error": "Process ... not found", "error_type":
"ProcessNotFound"}@{utt}.

@{h2}Being woken

With @{tt}wake: true@{utt} the session is woken when the command ends, so
the agent can end its turn instead of polling. The answer's
@{tt}wake@{utt} says whether the wake is armed; when it is not,
@{tt}wake_note@{utt} says why (waking is switched off, the call belongs
to no session, the wake chain is too deep) and the agent polls. An armed
wake is still best effort. The woken run is a new process: it reads the
outcome from a copy the old one kept -- the last 30,000 characters of
each stream, for an hour, handed over once. A command whose output was
read or that was stopped does not wake anybody.
@endnode

@node files "Files"
@{h1}Files

Both tools copy over SFTP on one of the machine's connections. Local paths
are on the server that runs the plugin, inside the folder
@{tt}local_root@{utt}; a relative one starts there. See
@{" What an agent can do " link security} for what is refused.

@{h2}ssh_control_upload_file

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
machine | the machine; required
local_path | a file in local_root; required
remote_path | where it goes on the machine, file name included; required
mode | permissions in octal, e.g. "0644"; set after the copy
@{body}

The answer has the same shape as @{tt}execute@{utt}'s: @{tt}results@{utt}
with @{tt}local_path@{utt} (resolved, absolute), @{tt}remote_path@{utt},
@{tt}bytes_transferred@{utt}, @{tt}duration@{utt} and
@{tt}success@{utt}, and the counts. A failure has @{tt}error@{utt}: "Local
file not found: ...", the SFTP error ("Permission denied", "No such
file"), or an invalid mode -- then the file is on the machine but its mode
is not set. Directories are not copied.

A local path the tools refuse fails the call itself, before any machine
is contacted: "Local files are disabled: local_root is not set. ...",
"Path is outside the allowed directories: ... Allowed: <folder>",
"local_path is a directory, not a file: ...", "Refusing a Windows
device name: ..." or "Path starts with '~' ...".

@{h2}ssh_control_download_file

Takes @{tt}machine@{utt}, @{tt}remote_path@{utt} and @{tt}local_path@{utt},
all required. It answers @{tt}machine@{utt}, @{tt}remote_path@{utt},
@{tt}local_path@{utt}, @{tt}bytes_transferred@{utt},
@{tt}duration@{utt}, @{tt}success: true@{utt} -- or @{tt}success:
false@{utt} with the @{tt}error@{utt}. A file at @{tt}local_path@{utt} is
replaced; missing folders inside local_root are created. A refused path
fails the call as for upload.

@{code json}
{"machine": "build-host", "local_path": "dist/app.tar.gz", "remote_path": "/srv/app/app.tar.gz", "mode": "0640"}
{"machine": "build-host", "remote_path": "/var/log/app/error.log", "local_path": "logs/build-host-error.log"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node machines "Machines and connections"
@{h1}Machines and connections

@{h2}ssh_control_list_machines

Every machine with @{tt}name@{utt}, @{tt}host@{utt}, @{tt}port@{utt},
@{tt}username@{utt}, @{tt}auth_method@{utt} and @{tt}tags@{utt}, and the
@{tt}count@{utt}. With @{tt}tags@{utt} only the machines that carry at
least one of them.

@{h2}ssh_control_check_connection

With @{tt}machine@{utt} one machine, without it every machine. It pings
each machine the plugin holds a connection to and answers per
machine @{tt}connected@{utt}, @{tt}latency_ms@{utt}, @{tt}error@{utt}
and @{tt}last_used@{utt}, plus @{tt}connected@{utt} and
@{tt}disconnected@{utt} counts. A machine without a connection -- not
used since the server started, or its connections lost -- is not
connected to for this: it answers @{tt}connected: false,
not_yet_connected: true@{utt}. To find out whether it can be reached, run
a command such as @{tt}true@{utt}. An unknown name answers
@{tt}error: "Machine not configured"@{utt}.

@{h2}ssh_control_add_machine

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
name | the new machine's name; required, must not exist yet
host, username | required
port | 22 when left out
auth_method | key (default), password or agent
key_path | for key: a key file on the server; ~/.ssh/id_rsa when left out
password | for password
tags | a list of labels for list_machines
max_connections | at least 1; 3 when left out
persistent | true: keep it across restarts
@{body}

The plugin connects and runs a test command first (10 seconds to connect,
5 to answer); only then is the machine added. It gets the default timeouts:
10 seconds to connect, 300 per command. The answer has
@{tt}success: true@{utt}, the machine's data, @{tt}persistent@{utt}
(whether it was stored) and @{tt}config_error@{utt} (why not); a refusal is
@{tt}success: false@{utt} with @{tt}error@{utt}: "Machine 'x' already
exists", "Connection timeout for x: 10 seconds to connect, 5 for the
test command", "max_connections must be a whole number of at least
1, not 0", "SSH connection
failed: ...".

@{tt}persistent@{utt} writes the machine to
@{tt}data/ssh_control/machines.<instance>.yaml@{utt} -- name, host,
port, user, login method, key path, tags, @{tt}max_connections@{utt}, never
a password. A password machine is therefore not stored
(@{tt}persistent: false@{utt}, @{tt}config_error@{utt} says why); it is
still added until the next restart.

@{h2}ssh_control_remove_machine

Takes @{tt}name@{utt} and @{tt}remove_from_config@{utt} (false by
default). It closes the machine's connections and removes it; with
@{tt}remove_from_config: true@{utt} also from the machine store. A machine
from the configuration is not in the store and comes back at the next
start (@{tt}config_error@{utt} says so).

@{h2}Connections

Each machine has a pool of at most @{tt}max_connections@{utt}
connections, opened on first use and kept for the next command. A command
that finds them all busy waits up to 30 seconds for one. Every connection
taken from and given back to the pool is checked with a quick
@{tt}echo 1@{utt}; one that does not answer is closed and replaced. A
connection that dies while it sits idle in the pool is not noticed until
the next command takes it.

A machine added at runtime gets the plugin's @{tt}defaults:@{utt} only
after a restart (when it was stored).
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Nine tools, named @{tt}<instance>_execute@{utt},
@{tt}_get_output@{utt}, @{tt}_kill_process@{utt},
@{tt}_upload_file@{utt}, @{tt}_download_file@{utt},
@{tt}_list_machines@{utt}, @{tt}_check_connection@{utt},
@{tt}_add_machine@{utt} and @{tt}_remove_machine@{utt}. Their descriptions
come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. The machine list is not in
them: an agent calls @{tt}list_machines@{utt}, or has the names in its
prompt. Errors are in the answer as described per tool; a missing required
parameter of execute, the transfers, get_output or kill_process, and a
refused local path, fail the call itself.

In the chat, the call's status line ends with its result, outcome first:
"build-host: exit 0 in 0.2s -- df -h /", "build-host: 0 ok, 1 failed
(Timed out after 300s) -- make test", "build-host: ssh_proc_3f9a1c2e
started (wakes this session) -- make all", "ssh_proc_3f9a1c2e on
build-host: exit 0, 18234 chars", "Uploaded: build-host: app.tar.gz →
/srv/app/app.tar.gz (2.1 MB)", "Added machine: staging (deploy\@staging.example:22)
(this session only)".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are ordinary tool results: appended, nothing earlier changes. The
plugin has no hook and never touches the prompt. A foreground command's
output comes back whole, and so does everything a background command has
buffered (up to 1000 lines per stream) on each @{tt}get_output@{utt} --
@{tt}clear_buffer: true@{utt} or @{tt}stream@{utt} keep repeated reads
small.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}No interactive commands: nothing can be typed into a running
command, and one that asks for input waits until its timeout.
@{bullet}A foreground command that times out gets no signal and may go
on running on the machine.
@{bullet}The panel's history and the background commands live in memory
only.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is configured where the agent that uses it is defined, with
its machines -- here in the agent's YAML under @{tt}config/agents/@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    ssh_control:
      type: ssh_control
      enabled: true
      local_root: data/ssh_control/files
      machines:
        - name: build-host
          host: build-host.example
          port: 22
          username: deploy
          auth_method: key
          key_path: ~/.ssh/build_host_ed25519
          max_connections: 3
          tags: [build]
        - name: staging
          host: 192.0.2.10
          username: ops
          auth_method: agent
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them -- all of them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+ssh_control/*"
@{body}

or only some, for an agent that should run commands on the configured
machines and do nothing else:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "ssh_control/ssh_control_execute"
      - "ssh_control/ssh_control_get_output"
      - "ssh_control/ssh_control_kill_process"
      - "ssh_control/ssh_control_list_machines"
      - "ssh_control/ssh_control_check_connection"
@{body}

The plugin needs @{tt}asyncssh@{utt}. Every host must be in the known_hosts
file of the account the server runs as (@{tt}ssh-keyscan
build-host.example >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts@{utt}, after checking the key),
and every key file must be readable by it.

@{h2}A machine

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
name | -- | how agents and the panel call it; required
host | -- | name or address; required
port | 22 | the SSH port
username | -- | required
auth_method | key | key, password or agent
key_path | -- | the private key; required for key
key_passphrase | -- | for an encrypted key
password | -- | required for password
connection_timeout | 10 | seconds to connect
command_timeout | 300 | seconds a foreground command may take
max_connections | 3 | connections at a time, at least 1; background commands take all but one
tags | [] | labels for list_machines
@{body}

@{tt}agent@{utt} leaves the login to asyncssh: the agent that
@{tt}SSH_AUTH_SOCK@{utt} names, and the default keys in
@{tt}~/.ssh@{utt}. A @{tt}defaults:@{utt} block next to
@{tt}machines:@{utt} gives every machine the keys it does not set itself
-- the machines of the configuration and the stored ones, read at start;
a machine added at runtime gets them only after a restart.
A machine entry that is invalid is logged and left out; the others load.

@{h2}Local files

@{tt}local_root@{utt} (no default) is the one folder
@{tt}upload_file@{utt} reads from and @{tt}download_file@{utt} writes to.
A relative value counts from the project folder, and @{tt}data/...@{utt}
from the data directory. Unset, empty or blank, both tools refuse every call and
say so -- the other tools work as before. The folder is not created at
start; a download creates what it needs inside it.

@{h2}Security settings

@{code yaml}
      security:
        strict_host_key_checking: true
        known_hosts_file: ~/.ssh/known_hosts
        audit_log: true
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
strict_host_key_checking | true | false: every host key is accepted; an empty value keeps it on
known_hosts_file | ~/.ssh/known_hosts | must exist while checking is on
audit_log | true | AUDIT lines in the server log; only false turns them off, see @{" What an agent can do " link security}
@{body}

@{h2}Where data lives

Machines added with @{tt}persistent@{utt} are in
@{tt}data/ssh_control/machines.<instance>.yaml@{utt} and are read at
start. A machine of the same name in the configuration wins; the stored
one is ignored with a warning. Recorded outcomes for woken runs are in the
plugin cache (@{tt}data/cache/<instance>/@{utt}) for an hour. Transferred
files are wherever @{tt}local_root@{utt} points (the sysadmin agent uses
@{tt}data/ssh_control/files@{utt}). Machines
that an old version wrote to @{tt}config/mcp.yaml@{utt} are not read;
at start the server log says how many there are and in which file, so
they can be added again.
@endnode
